(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-decongestants-cough-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Decongestants, Antitussives & Expectorants Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is N-acetylcysteine traditionally used for, apart from its mucolytic action?","choices":["Aspirin poisoning","Acetaminophen poisoning","Opioid overdose","Organophosphate poisoning"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same drug turns up in two entirely unrelated places -- as an antidote in poisoning and as a mucolytic in the airway -- which is worth noticing rather than being surprised by. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What congestion is pseudoephedrine indicated to relieve?","choices":["Nasal and sinus congestion from a cold","Congestion from bacterial sinusitis only","Chest congestion with productive cough","Congestion from nasal polyps only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It covers both infective and allergic causes, because it acts on the swollen vessels rather than on the cause of the swelling. Hay fever or allergy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is a patient starting guaifenesin told to expect?","choices":["Less drainage within a day","A bitter taste","More drainage","Drowsiness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Warning the patient that drainage increases stops them reading the drug working as the drug failing. To drink plenty of fluid. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which allergy history is a contraindication to benzonatate?","choices":["Allergy to penicillin or a cephalosporin","Allergy to aspirin or other salicylates","Allergy to iodine or contrast media","Allergy to tetracaine or related anesthetics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is chemically related to the ester local anesthetics, so a reaction to one predicts a reaction to the other. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What risk arises when dextromethorphan is combined with other serotonergic drugs?","choices":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Hypertensive crisis alone","Serotonin syndrome","Prolonged bleeding time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An over-the-counter cough medicine can supply the second serotonergic drug, which is exactly why it is worth asking about. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How does dornase alfa reduce sputum viscosity?","choices":["It cleaves DNA released from degenerating neutrophils","It splits disulfide bonds between mucoproteins","It draws water onto the airway surface","It suppresses neutrophil recruitment to the lung"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The thickness comes from the DNA of dead white cells, so an enzyme that cuts DNA is a precisely targeted answer to it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is a patient on guaifenesin told to follow up?","choices":["Only if a fever develops","After a single day without improvement","Never -- it is available without prescription","If symptoms persist beyond about a week or get worse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A cough that outlasts the self-treatment window needs a cause found rather than more expectorant. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does pseudoephedrine differ from oxymetazoline in mechanism and route?","choices":["It blocks histamine receptors and is taken orally","It is an alpha antagonist given orally","Same alpha agonist mechanism, but taken orally","It is a topical steroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mechanism is shared; taking it orally is what changes both its usefulness and its side effect profile. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Besides rebound congestion, what systemic effect is described with oxymetazoline?","choices":["A slow heart rate","Raised blood pressure","Drowsiness and sedation","Hypoglycaemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A drug sprayed into the nose still reaches the circulation, which is what makes it worth asking about in a hypertensive patient. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with guaifenesin?","choices":["Confusion and agitation","Hypertension and tachycardia","Hepatic failure","Nausea and vomiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A short list, which is part of why it is available without prescription. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What activates cough receptors in the respiratory tract?","choices":["Mechanical stretch alone","Changes in temperature alone","Chemical and mechanical irritants","Release of histamine alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both kinds, which is why a cough can come from a fume, a crumb or a drip of mucus equally. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How does benzonatate suppress cough?","choices":["It suppresses the medullary cough center","It anesthetizes stretch receptors in the lungs","It thins mucus so there is less to clear","It blocks histamine receptors in the airway"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It works at the start of the reflex rather than in the brain, which is the whole contrast with dextromethorphan. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which medicines interact with oxymetazoline?","choices":["Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and antidepressants","Antibiotics of any class or route","Oral and nasal antihistamine preparations","Proton pump inhibitors and other antacid drugs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both raise the effect of a sympathomimetic, so an over-the-counter spray becomes a blood pressure problem in these patients. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Besides improving lung function, what else does dornase alfa achieve?","choices":["It eradicates chronic airway infection","It reduces the number of exacerbations","It replaces the need for airway clearance physiotherapy","It reverses established lung damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fewer episodes matters as much as a better spirometry number, and arguably more to the patient. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which are named as the less common complications of coughing?","choices":["Dysrhythmias, syncope, stroke and rib fracture","Hoarseness, insomnia and exhaustion","Bronchospasm, wheeze and breathlessness together","Nosebleed and nasal congestion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rare, but they establish that a cough can do structural and vascular harm, not merely wear a patient down. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What kind of cough is benzonatate indicated for?","choices":["A productive cough with thick sputum","Cough from bacterial pneumonia","Any cough regardless of character","A non-productive cough"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suppressing a cough that is clearing something would be working against the patient. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How does oxymetazoline relieve nasal congestion?","choices":["It is an alpha agonist that constricts vessels in the nasal mucosa","It blocks histamine H1 receptors in the mucosa","It thins mucus so it drains more easily","It reduces inflammation through a glucocorticoid receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Congestion is swollen mucosa rather than mucus, so shrinking the vessels is what opens the nose. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient treated for hypertension asks about pseudoephedrine for a cold. What is the concern?","choices":["It increases the effect of antihypertensives, causing hypotension","It reduces the effect of antihypertensive medicines","It has no effect on blood pressure treatment","It cannot be taken with any antibiotic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient can undo their blood pressure treatment with something bought off the shelf, without ever mentioning it as a medicine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
